<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murray MacLehose</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/2000">2000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lord MacLehose of Beoch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murray MacLehose was a British diplomat who served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> from 1971 to 1982, the longest tenure of any governor. He launched a large public housing programme, expanded education and social services, and in 1974 set up the Independent Commission Against Corruption. He also raised the question of Hong Kong's future with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1979, opening the way to the negotiations of the 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murray-maclehose.jpg" alt="Murray MacLehose" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aplcwche · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HK_Wong_Pao_Yue-Kong_Swimming_Pool_Complex_Foundation_Stone_by_Murray_MacLehose_1977.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Crawford Murray MacLehose was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> on 16 October 1917, the son of Hamish Alexander MacLehose, who owned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> business, and Margaret Bruce Black. He went to Rugby School and Balliol College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he read modern history, and joined the colonial administration of British Malaya in 1939. Sent to the consulate in Xiamen in 1940 to learn Hokkien, he trained Chinese guerrillas for sabotage behind Japanese lines under cover as vice-consul, was detained by the Japanese in December 1941 and repatriated in 1942, then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with British naval intelligence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the war he was acting consul in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> and from 1948 consul general in Hankou, where he learned Mandarin. He was principal private secretary to the Foreign Secretary George Brown and ambassador to South Vietnam from 1967 to 1969, surviving a lapse when he left a confidential telegram between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson">Harold Wilson</a> and Lyndon Johnson in a bank. He became governor in November 1971, after the 1967 riots, and served until May 1982, ten years and six months.</p><p>His reforms made Chinese an official language beside English, expanded welfare, began the Ten-year Housing Programme in 1972, introduced nine years of compulsory education, and built new towns such as Sha Tin and Tuen Mun, the Country Parks and the Mass Transit Railway. He set up District Boards and, under strong public pressure, the ICAC. In his first two years government spending rose by over 50%. He was convinced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> would reclaim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and opposed moves towards constitutional democracy. In 1979, as the first governor to visit the People's Republic officially, he raised the lease of the New Territories with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He is generally regarded as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>'s most popular governors, and the 1970s became known as the MacLehose Era. Members of the pro-democracy camp, among them Martin Lee, criticised him for not pressing for democratic reform, while he insisted he had not given Hong Kong away. He became a life peer as Baron MacLehose of Beoch in 1982 and a Knight of the Thistle in 1983. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a> on 27 May 2000, and the 100-kilometre MacLehose Trail bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murray MacLehose, Baron MacLehose of Beoch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor <b>Murray MacLehose</b> expanded housing and created the anti-corruption commission in 1974.</blockquote><a href="/state/hong-kong-british">Hong Kong (British)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.86,-4.25&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Glasgow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4457 260 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Murray MacLehose"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4907 530h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4907 530h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.86,-4.25&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
